blocks:
  - type: metin
    metin: Famagusta welcomes university students from more than 100 countries. That diversity enriches campus life, but it also means each student faces a unique set of challenges around adjustment and academic pressure. Knowing where to turn when things get hard makes a real difference.
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: Why do students' mental health often suffer at university?
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Adjustment difficulties in a new environment
        metin: Leaving family for the first time, starting out in an unfamiliar city or country, building new friendships — combined, these create a heavy adjustment burden.
      - baslik: Academic pressure
        metin: Midterms, finals, project deadlines and grade anxiety — especially when a scholarship or visa depends on academic performance — can tip into chronic stress.
      - baslik: Loneliness and homesickness
        metin: Social media exaggerates how much fun everyone else is having. Meanwhile, you may be sitting alone in your dorm room. That contradiction deepens homesickness and low mood.
      - baslik: Identity and uncertainty about the future
        metin: Regret about your choice of degree, career uncertainty, wrestling with "who am I?" — the existential questions that surface at university carry real psychological weight.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: Seeing a psychologist does not mean you are "crazy"
    metin: This stigma still stops many students from seeking support. Yet therapy is an evidence-based form of support for everyday challenges such as adjustment difficulties, [anxiety](/en/blog/what-is-anxiety-disorder.html), low motivation and relationship issues. Asking for help is a sign of strength, not weakness.
